WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.140
Same hormone, same monkey.

2
00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:09.460
Could the timing of a signal matter as much as its amount?

3
00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:15.120
The first film showed GnRH reaching the pituitary in pulses.

4
00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:18.840
This film asks whether the pulse is the message.

5
00:00:19.950 --> 00:00:23.690
Ernst Knobil's laboratory worked with female rhesus monkeys.

6
00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:29.670
Damage to the hypothalamus, called a lesion, had stopped their LH and FSH.

7
00:00:31.070 --> 00:00:33.410
But the pituitary itself was still intact.

8
00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:35.930
The design is simple.

9
00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:39.470
Remove the animal's own source of a hormone.

10
00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:45.450
Then give it back from outside on different schedules, with the steady one giving at

11
00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:46.570
least as much in total.

12
00:00:47.683 --> 00:00:50.363
It also needed a clean supply of the hormone.

13
00:00:51.823 --> 00:00:53.803
GnRH had just been isolated.

14
00:00:55.223 --> 00:00:59.143
Pure synthetic GnRH made this kind of experiment possible.

15
00:01:00.267 --> 00:01:02.907
So the team could give GnRH through a vein.

16
00:01:04.167 --> 00:01:06.987
They read the pituitary's answer in blood samples.

17
00:01:08.207 --> 00:01:11.607
With no drive from above, the LH record started flat.

18
00:01:13.817 --> 00:01:16.717
First, brief infusions, once every hour.

19
00:01:17.917 --> 00:01:21.337
The pump gives a short burst, then stops, then bursts again.

20
00:01:22.777 --> 00:01:27.417
Hourly pulses restored LH and FSH secretion, and kept it going.

21
00:01:28.467 --> 00:01:30.587
Now switch the pump to a steady stream.

22
00:01:31.867 --> 00:01:36.647
Set it high enough that each day it delivers at least as much GnRH as the pulses.

23
00:01:38.067 --> 00:01:40.707
Will LH stay up, rise higher, or fall away?

24
00:01:46.767 --> 00:01:52.407
It falls away. The steady supply did not sustain LH and FSH.

25
00:01:53.787 --> 00:01:58.627
If the pituitary counted only the total, the steady drip would have done at least as

26
00:01:58.627 --> 00:02:00.867
well. It did not.

27
00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:04.180
Then the team went back to hourly pulses.

28
00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:08.200
The authors reported that secretion came back.

29
00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:11.440
So the pituitary had not been damaged.

30
00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:15.140
It could still answer, when the timing was right.

31
00:02:16.150 --> 00:02:20.870
So the pituitary reads the timing of GnRH, not only its total.

32
00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:23.810
Hourly pulses keep it working.

33
00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:27.630
A steady supply, even a larger one, did not.

34
00:02:29.083 --> 00:02:30.803
This experiment has limits.

35
00:02:32.083 --> 00:02:33.783
It shows the rule in these monkeys.

36
00:02:35.043 --> 00:02:39.883
It does not show where the pulses come from, or what interval between pulses is

37
00:02:39.883 --> 00:02:44.343
normal in people. Nor does it show why a steady supply fails.
